---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/indas/indas103.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Identifying the Acquirer & Acquisition Date" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The Acquisition Method — Four Steps" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Recognising Identifiable Assets & Liabilities" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Goodwill & Bargain Purchase — Capital Reserve" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Common Control Combinations — Pooling of Interests" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Non-Controlling Interests" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Ind AS 103 vs IFRS 3 — Full Comparison" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 — CONCEPTUAL FRAMEWORK LINKAGE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Convergence with a genuinely important carve-out for group reorganisations</div>
        <div class="principle">Ind AS 103 applies the SAME acquisition method as IFRS 3 for arm's-length business combinations. However, India's corporate landscape features EXTENSIVE group reorganisations (mergers between companies under COMMON promoter/parent control) — transactions with NO genuine change in ultimate ownership. Applying the acquisition method's fair value re-measurement to such transactions would arguably NOT faithfully represent their economic substance, since NO new economic transaction with an outside party has genuinely occurred. Ind AS 103 addresses this with a DEDICATED "pooling of interests" method for common control combinations — an area IFRS 3 leaves entirely UNADDRESSED.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IND AS 103 MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Substance over form</strong> — a genuine ARM'S-LENGTH acquisition (fair value re-measurement, goodwill recognition) is economically DIFFERENT from an internal group reorganisation with no change in ultimate control — Ind AS 103 reflects this distinction directly, unlike IFRS 3</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Faithful representation</strong> — applying the acquisition method (with its fair value step-ups and goodwill) to a common control transaction would arguably OVERSTATE the economic significance of what is, in substance, merely an internal restructuring</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">A genuinely important Indian carve-out</strong> — this is one of the MOST SIGNIFICANT and frequently applied differences between Ind AS and IFRS, given the prevalence of Indian group restructurings (e.g. under the Companies Act's scheme of arrangement provisions)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IFRS 3 EXPLICITLY EXCLUDES common control combinations from its scope, leaving the accounting treatment to each jurisdiction's own guidance — Ind AS 103 FILLS this gap directly with its own prescribed pooling of interests method, a genuinely IMPORTANT and DISTINCTIVE feature not found in global IFRS at all.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Two fundamentally different methods, by transaction type</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Transaction type</span><span>Method applied</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Arm's-length acquisition</span><span>ACQUISITION method — fair value, goodwill, IDENTICAL to IFRS 3</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Common control combination</span><span>POOLING OF INTERESTS method — book values, NO goodwill, capital reserve for any difference</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info" style="margin-bottom:0;">ℹ️ This DUAL-method structure is the SINGLE most important distinguishing feature of Ind AS 103 relative to IFRS 3.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 — SCOPE & APPLICABILITY ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What Ind AS 103 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">Ind AS 103 applies to ALL business combinations, EXPLICITLY including common control combinations (unlike IFRS 3, which is silent on them) — reflecting India's own comprehensive approach to filling this gap left by global IFRS.</div>
        <div class="note">A "business" definition and the "asset acquisition vs business combination" distinction are SUBSTANTIALLY aligned with IFRS 3.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Common control business combination</div><div class="glossary-def">A business combination involving entities/businesses ALREADY controlled by the SAME party (or parties), both before and after the combination — that control being NOT transitory.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Pooling of interests method</div><div class="glossary-def">The mandatory method for common control combinations — assets/liabilities recorded at PREVIOUS carrying amounts, no fair value step-up, no goodwill.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Capital reserve</div><div class="glossary-def">The equity line item recording any difference arising in a common control combination (an Ind AS-specific concept, distinct from IFRS 3's goodwill/bargain purchase treatment).</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 — IDENTIFYING THE ACQUIRER & ACQUISITION DATE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Identifying the Acquirer &amp; Acquisition Date</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Identical to IFRS 3 for arm's-length combinations</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">For NON-common-control combinations, Ind AS 103 identifies the acquirer using IFRS 10-equivalent (Ind AS 110) control guidance, IDENTICAL to IFRS 3 — including reverse acquisition scenarios where the legal acquirer is the accounting acquiree.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 — THE ACQUISITION METHOD ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Acquisition Method — Four Steps</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Identical to IFRS 3</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Step 1 — Identify the acquirer</span>
          <span>Step 2 — Determine the acquisition date</span>
          <span>Step 3 — Recognise/measure identifiable assets/liabilities and NCI</span>
          <span>Step 4 — Recognise goodwill or a bargain purchase gain</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">This four-step sequence applies UNCHANGED for genuine arm's-length combinations under Ind AS 103.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 — RECOGNISING IDENTIFIABLE ASSETS & LIABILITIES ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Recognising Identifiable Assets &amp; Liabilities</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Identical fair value approach to IFRS 3</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">For arm's-length combinations, identifiable assets/liabilities are measured at ACQUISITION-DATE FAIR VALUE — IDENTICAL to IFRS 3, including the specific override recognising contingent liabilities at fair value even below IAS 37/Ind AS 37's normal "probable" threshold.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 — GOODWILL & BARGAIN PURCHASE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Goodwill &amp; Bargain Purchase — "Capital Reserve" Terminology (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Same formula as IFRS 3, with an Indian terminology nuance</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-eq">Goodwill = Consideration transferred + Fair value of NCI + Fair value of previously held interest − Fair value of net identifiable assets acquired</div>
        </div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Bargain purchase recognised as Capital Reserve</div>
          <div class="wk-body">An Indian company acquires 100% of a target for ₹40 crore. Fair value of net identifiable assets acquired: ₹48 crore.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Excess</strong> = 48 − 40 = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-success);">₹8 crore</strong> — after mandatory reassessment (identical to IFRS 3's requirement), this bargain purchase gain is recognised in <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">"Capital Reserve"</strong> — this specific EQUITY-based terminology and presentation choice for the gain is a distinctly INDIAN accounting convention, reflecting long-standing Indian company law practice around "capital" versus "revenue" reserves, whereas IFRS 3 simply recognises the equivalent bargain purchase gain in PROFIT OR LOSS.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-bottom:0;">⚠️ <strong style="font-weight:600">This is a genuine, substantive difference:</strong> Ind AS 103 recognises the bargain purchase gain in a SEPARATE EQUITY reserve (Capital Reserve), NOT in profit or loss — a notable divergence from IFRS 3's profit-or-loss treatment.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 — COMMON CONTROL COMBINATIONS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Common Control Business Combinations — Pooling of Interests (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Ind AS 103's most distinctive feature</div>
        <div class="principle">For business combinations involving entities UNDER COMMON CONTROL (e.g. merger of two subsidiaries of the same parent, or a parent merging with its subsidiary), Ind AS 103 MANDATES the POOLING OF INTERESTS method: assets and liabilities of the combining entities are recorded at their EXISTING (previous) CARRYING VALUES, with NO fair value step-up and NO goodwill recognised. Any DIFFERENCE between the consideration and the net asset value acquired is adjusted DIRECTLY in the "Capital Reserve" or "General Reserve" within EQUITY — never through profit or loss, and comparative figures are RESTATED as if the combination had occurred from the beginning of the earliest period presented.</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 2: A common control merger</div>
          <div class="wk-body">Company A and Company B are both wholly owned subsidiaries of the same Indian parent, Company P. Company P merges B into A. B's net assets, at PREVIOUS carrying value (book value), total ₹25 crore. A issues shares valued at ₹28 crore to B's (parent-controlled) shareholders as merger consideration.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">Since A and B are under COMMON CONTROL (both wholly owned by P, both before and after), the POOLING OF INTERESTS method applies: A recognises B's net assets at their EXISTING book value of ₹25 crore (NO fair value step-up, NO goodwill). The ₹3 crore difference (28 − 25) is adjusted DIRECTLY against <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">Capital Reserve within equity</strong> — comparative financial statements are ALSO RESTATED to reflect the combined entity as if it had always existed, a treatment with NO parallel in IFRS 3 at all.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 — NON-CONTROLLING INTERESTS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Non-Controlling Interests</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Identical choice to IFRS 3</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">For arm's-length combinations, Ind AS 103 offers the SAME choice as IFRS 3 — measuring NCI at FAIR VALUE (full goodwill method) or at its PROPORTIONATE SHARE of identifiable net assets (partial goodwill method), elected separately for each combination.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 — DISCLOSURE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Largely identical to IFRS 3, plus common control specific disclosures</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Name/description of acquiree, acquisition date, consideration transferred, goodwill/capital reserve amount — identical to IFRS 3</span>
          <span>For COMMON CONTROL combinations SPECIFICALLY: description of the method used, the fact of restatement, and the amount adjusted to capital reserve/general reserve</span>
          <span>NCI measurement method chosen, per combination</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 — JUDGMENTS & ESTIMATES ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Whether a combination genuinely qualifies as "COMMON CONTROL" — requiring control to be NOT transitory and genuinely the SAME ultimate controlling party, both before and after</span>
              <span>Whether an acquisition constitutes a "business" — identical judgement to IFRS 3</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Fair value of identifiable assets/liabilities for arm's-length combinations — identical to IFRS 3</span>
              <span>Fair value of contingent consideration</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">⚠️ Determining whether a transaction genuinely meets the "COMMON CONTROL" definition (particularly the "not transitory" requirement) is one of the MOST significant and frequently litigated judgements under Ind AS 103, given the SIGNIFICANTLY different accounting outcomes (fair value + goodwill vs book value + capital reserve) depending on the classification.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 — IND AS 103 vs IFRS 3 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Ind AS 103 vs IFRS 3 — Full Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comprehensive comparison table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS 3</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-success);">Ind AS 103</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Arm's-length combinations</span><span>Acquisition method — fair value, goodwill</span><span>IDENTICAL acquisition method</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Common control combinations</span><span>OUTSIDE IFRS 3's scope — left to local guidance/policy choice</span><span>MANDATORY, dedicated "pooling of interests" method — book values, no goodwill, capital reserve adjustment, comparative restatement — a MAJOR, distinctive Ind AS feature</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Bargain purchase gain presentation</span><span>Recognised in PROFIT OR LOSS</span><span>Recognised in <strong>Capital Reserve within EQUITY</strong> — a genuine, substantive presentation difference</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>NCI measurement choice</span><span>Fair value or proportionate share, per combination</span><span>IDENTICAL choice</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Measurement period</span><span>Up to 1 year from acquisition date</span><span>IDENTICAL</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">The COMMON CONTROL carve-out and the Capital Reserve (rather than P&amp;L) presentation of bargain purchase gains are the TWO most significant, substantive differences between Ind AS 103 and IFRS 3 — reflecting genuinely important features of the INDIAN corporate and legal environment (frequent group reorganisations, and Indian company law's historical capital/revenue reserve distinction) that global IFRS 3 does not address.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 — STATEMENT MAPPING ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Arm's-length acquisition — assets/liabilities at fair value</span><span><span class="badge bs">Balance Sheet</span></span><span>IDENTICAL to IFRS 3</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Goodwill recognised (arm's-length)</span><span><span class="badge bs">Balance Sheet</span></span><span>Tested annually for impairment, same as IFRS 3</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Bargain purchase gain</span><span><span class="badge be">Equity (Capital Reserve)</span></span><span>NOT profit or loss — the key Ind AS divergence</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Common control combination — assets/liabilities at book value</span><span><span class="badge bs">Balance Sheet</span></span><span>No fair value step-up; comparatives restated</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Common control difference (consideration vs net assets)</span><span><span class="badge be">Equity (Capital/General Reserve)</span></span><span>Never through profit or loss</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Acquisition-related costs</span><span><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span></span><span>Expensed as incurred, identical to IFRS 3</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 — RELATED STANDARDS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 3</span><span>The GLOBAL counterpart standard, converged for arm's-length combinations only</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Ind AS 110</span><span>Supplies the control definition determining the acquirer and identifying common control</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Ind AS 36</span><span>Governs annual goodwill impairment testing for arm's-length combinations</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Companies Act, 2013 (Sections 230-232)</span><span>Governs the LEGAL scheme of arrangement process through which many Indian common control mergers are effected, ALONGSIDE Ind AS 103's accounting treatment</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Pooling of interests method</div><div class="glossary-def">The Ind AS 103-specific mandatory method for common control combinations, using book values with no goodwill.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Capital reserve</div><div class="glossary-def">An equity reserve used in Ind AS 103 for both bargain purchase gains (arm's-length combinations) and the balancing figure in common control combinations — a distinctly Indian presentation convention.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 — GLOSSARY ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Common control business combination", definition: "A business combination involving entities/businesses ALREADY controlled by the SAME party (or parties), both before and after the combination — that control being NOT transitory." },
      { term: "Pooling of interests method", definition: "The mandatory method for common control combinations — assets/liabilities recorded at PREVIOUS carrying amounts, no fair value step-up, no goodwill." },
      { term: "Capital reserve", definition: "The equity line item recording any difference arising in a common control combination (an Ind AS-specific concept, distinct from IFRS 3's goodwill/bargain purchase treatment)." },
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
